Check .env against .env.example, find issues

Usage: evnx validate [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --input-format <FORMAT>
          How to read `--env`: `dotenv` or `json`.
          
          `json` takes a flat `{"KEY": "value"}` object, or what `evnx parse --format json --values` prints. It is never guessed: `.env` text may start with `{`.

          Possible values:
          - dotenv: `.env` text
          - json:   A flat `{"KEY": "value"}` object
          
          [default: dotenv]

      --strict
          Also report variables that are present but not declared.
          
          Measured against `.env.example`, or against `[vars]` when the project has a contract. Without it, an undeclared variable is not mentioned.

      --fix
          Repair what can be repaired, instead of only reporting it.
          
          Fills missing variables, replaces placeholder values and corrects boolean traps. Only `.env` is written, the template is never touched, and the original is kept beside it as `.env.bak`.
          
          A missing variable gets the template's value. A new secret is generated only for one the app holds alone — its own signing key (`SECRET_KEY`, `JWT_SECRET`, `SESSION_SECRET`, …) or a password; a key a provider issues (`STRIPE_KEY`, an AWS secret, a token) gets a placeholder, because evnx cannot invent it. Those are listed under "Needs your input", not as fixed, and the run exits 1 until they are filled in.
          
          ⛔ **It will not overwrite a value that is not a placeholder.** A weak secret with a real value behind it is reported and left alone — see `--rotate-weak-secrets`.

  -n, --dry-run
          With `--fix`, show what it would change and write nothing.
          
          No `.env` change and no backup. The report and the exit code are those of the file as it stands; a secret `--fix` would generate is not shown, because the real run makes a new one.

      --rotate-weak-secrets
          With `--fix`, also replace weak secrets that hold a **real value**.
          
          ⛔ **This destroys credentials, and that is what it is for.** Until 2026-10-07 `--fix` did it unasked: `DATABASE_PASSWORD="p@ss word#1"` became `DATABASE_PASSWORD=ad0da713…c508`, and the run reported "All checks passed".
          
          The check behind it judges length and wording. It cannot tell a placeholder nobody filled in from the real password, short because someone chose a short one — and the second is the common case, because shortness is what trips it. Whether anything *else* holds the credential is not a fact in the file, so evnx cannot decide it; you can.
          
          Rotate at the provider first. A new value here that the provider does not have is an outage, not a repair — so a key a provider issues is never replaced, even with this flag: only the app's own secrets and passwords are.

      --exit-zero
          Always exit 0, even when problems are found.
          
          For a step that should report without failing the build. It suppresses the *findings* exit code only — a file that cannot be read still exits 2, because "I found nothing" and "I could not look" are different answers.

      --ignore <IGNORE>
          Comma-separated list of issue types to ignore
          
          Added to `[validate] ignore` in `.evnx.toml`. An unknown type is an error that lists the valid ones, so a typo cannot silence nothing.

      --validate-formats
          Validate value formats: url, port, email

      --fail-on <LEVEL>
          The lowest severity that fails the run: `error` or `warning`.
          
          Defaults to `error`, or `[validate] fail_on` from `.evnx.toml`. `warning` makes warnings exit 1 too, for gates that want them fixed. `--exit-zero` still wins.

          Possible values:
          - error:   Only errors fail the run
          - warning: Warnings fail it too

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Files:
      --env <FILE>
          Path to the environment file to check, or `-` to read stdin. Defaults to `.env`, or `[defaults] env_name` from `.evnx.toml`

      --env-name <NAME>
          Use `.env.<NAME>` instead of `.env`

      --example <FILE>
          The template to compare against. Defaults to `.env.example`, or `[defaults] example` from `.evnx.toml`

Output:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format. `json` is the machine-readable form, with a `summary` object for `jq`. `github` emits `::error` annotations that appear inline on a pull request.
          
          [default: pretty]
          [possible values: pretty, json, github]

      --json
          Same as --format json

Confirmation:
  -y, --yes
          With `--fix`, apply the fixes without asking.
          
          `--fix` never stops to ask, so this changes nothing today; it is accepted so a script can say so, and holds if a confirmation is ever added.

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/validate
